00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a big victory for the gop  in the house this week republican nicole  malliotakis flipping new york's 11th  congressional district red republicans  have flipped  10 seats in the house with eight of them  won by female candidates  the congresswoman-elect nicole  meliotanka joins us now nicole welcome  to the show and congratulations on your  victory  uh first and foremost what do you think  was the chief reason for that victory uh  that is my um you know i grew up in  staten island and i heard from a lot of  people  that it was the defund the police  movement on the left that really  terrified them  look our city is moving more and more to  the left and people are seeing crime  increasing quality life deteriorating  our mayor keeps hammering us over the  head with high taxes so people had  enough of a one-party democratic rule  and this was their opportunity to speak  out and make sure that new york has at  least  one republican representing our city in  washington  tell me about this freedom squad that  you have an idea for  you know we don't have a name yet  because we're not in high school but i  got to tell you that  uh there is a natural alliance that is  occurring here and we are bonded  by the fact that we come from different  experiences  of socialism my mother was a cuban  refugee who fled  the castro regime in 1959 carlos jimenez  is  cuban born he came to the america at the  age of six  we also have victoria spartz who comes  from the ukraine  grew up under soviet union rule and you  also have maria elvira  salazar who is like me the daughter of  cuban refugees and so  uh we have a natural alliance that is  forming and we are bonded by our love of  america  a love for freedom and liberty and the  opportunity that the american dream  represents and we want to preserve that  and we must push back against the forces  who are trying to destroy that  you know one of the challenges that  you're going to face when it comes to  issues related particularly to new york  is this massive  exodus from new york city amid the  coronavirus amid an uptick in crime it's  been pretty astounding if  if you look at these numbers new york  city locals leaving over the past eight  months 295  103 change of address request from march  1st through october 31st 2020.  what do you think about that and what  could be done to rectify that and bring  new york back  and this goes back to the original point  people seeing crime  increasing this wacky bail law that took  effect that led to  sharp increase in murder and shootings  and and  burglaries as well as car thefts people  feel less safe in the community and also  the quality of life is deteriorating  with these massive shutdowns as you know  broadway is shut down and you can't go  to madison square garden and our  restaurants are suffering the stores are  being boarded up because they're afraid  of looters  and so people are generally concerned so  we have to ensure that we  are having a real dialogue with mayor de  blasio and governor cuomo about the  impacts that their shutdowns and  restrictions are having  on our economy and making sure that we  have a balanced approach  to reopening of course public safety is  paramount  but we can't destroy our city and drive  people  out or else we're going to be in a very  very bad situation  come this time next year so for me it's  about providing a counter view  to what the mayor and the governor are  doing and ensuring that we work together  to find common ground to make sure that  we keep our economy open and vibrant and  we can keep new yorkers here because the  middle class has had enough  and they are leaving in droves  yeah we didn't get to it today but  obviously we also have that 10 pm curfew  that's going to be rolling in on  restaurants and other industries maybe  we'll get to that next time but  congratulations on your win and we'll  talk more next time nicole  appreciate it thank you thanks so much

